---

On Saturday, April 1st, over 200 Embakasi residents packed the Embakasi Community Centre to tell Bill Llia exactly what's broken with Nairobi County's healthcare — and what they expect the next Governor to fix.

## What we heard

The message was clear and urgent. Residents named the failures they face every day:

- **Drug stock-outs** at public health facilities force patients to buy essential medicines at private pharmacies — or go without
- **Staffing shortages** mean two-hour queues at understaffed clinics, with some nurses covering three wards
- **Maternal health gaps** leave mothers delivering in facilities that lack emergency obstetric care
- **NHIF coverage** remains out of reach for too many families, pushing healthcare costs onto household budgets already stretched thin

## Bill's response

Bill didn't sugarcoat it. "I've lived these problems alongside you — I've been in those clinics, I've waited in those queues," he told the crowd. "As Governor, I will put a community health center in every ward. I will staff every facility and stock every pharmacy. And I will work with the national government to bring health insurance within reach of every Nairobi family."

This forum is the first of a county-wide engagement series Bill is running in every constituency before the election.

**Upcoming events — mark your calendar:**
- April 15 — Kamukunji Youth Sports Centre (Education & Youth)
- April 22 — Kasarani Community Hall (Economic Empowerment)
- April 29 — Westlands (Water & Infrastructure)

All events are free. Show up. Be heard. This is your chance to shape the agenda for the next Governor.

## The takeaway for Nairobi voters

The next Governor will decide whether your clinic has medicine, whether your local hospital has a doctor, and whether your family can afford care. These forums are your opportunity to hold the candidate accountable before election day. Don't wait until after the vote to demand answers.
